{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Abraham was old, advanced in days.</b> The verse explains that because he was old, he feared for himself lest he die before they would wed Isaac; and he did not want to send the servant there without an oath, lest he die before he returned from the mission, and therefore he adjured his servant — for he knew of Isaac that he would heed his counsel and not transgress the words of his mouth. And the reason he did not adjure Isaac in this matter is that had he adjured him, the servant would have deceived him with his arguments and stratagems, for Canaan's balance is in his hand with deceit (Hosea 12:8), and to strengthen the matter further he adjured the servant." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> On account of four things old age leaps upon a person: on account of fear, on account of the anger of children, on account of a bad wife, on account of wars. On account of fear — from where do we know? As it is said (1 Chronicles 21:30): ״And David could not go before it to inquire of Elohim, for he was terrified because of the sword of the angel of YHWH.״ And it is written (1 Kings): ״And King David was old, advanced in days.״ On account of the anger of children, as it is said (1 Samuel 2:22): ״And Eli was very old, and he heard all that his sons did.״ On account of the wars — after the wars that Joshua waged it is written (Joshua 13:1): ״And Joshua was old, advanced in days.״ On account of a bad wife, as it is said (1 Kings 11:4): ״And it came to pass, when Solomon was old, that his wives turned away his heart.״ But Abraham — his wife honors him, as it is said: ״And my master is old.״" "<b>And YHWH had blessed Abraham בכל.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), its meaning is 'in every thing,' that is, that he was complete in everything — in wealth and possessions and honor and length of days; nothing was lacking to his soul of all that he might desire, except only that he might see children for his children, and because he desired this he adjured his servant to go on this mission." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״And YHWH had blessed Abraham בכל״ — Rabbi Meir says: he did not have a daughter. Rabbi Judah says: he did have a daughter. Others say: he had a daughter, and her name was Bakol. Rabbi Meir intended to say that the blessing is that he did not have a daughter, because he could not have married her off except to the children of Canaan, the cursed seed; and even if he were to marry her to the people of his native land, there too she would be engaged in idolatry among them, for a wife is under the authority of her husband. And Rabbi Judah intended to say that he did have a daughter, and even a daughter the Merciful One did not withhold from him — and that is the blessing בכל, for he had everything that it is in human nature to desire: sons and daughters. And the intent of the Others in saying 'he had a daughter and her name was Bakol' revealed to us in this statement a great secret, and explained that the intent of this blessing is not in whether he had a daughter or did not have a daughter; the intent is not a blessing of bodily matters at all, but a blessing in the affairs of the soul." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> The explanation of the matter is that Abraham, peace be upon him, merited and attained the attribute whose name is Kol, through which all who are blessed by it are blessed in heaven and on earth. And from this the Others said 'he had a daughter' — that is, he had an attribute, in the language of (1 Kings 7:26): ״אלפים בת יכיל״ — 'two thousand bat it held.' And they said 'and her name was Bakol,' because she is within the attribute called Kol, emanated from the attribute whose name is Kol; and this emanation is called 'a daughter,' and she is the court of the Holy One, blessed be He. And therefore it says ״וה׳ ברך״ — 'and YHWH blessed' — and it did not say ״וברך ה׳״. And grasp this principle, for the matter is hidden. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, revealed it explicitly and expanded in its explanation more than enough."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "The word כעס appears in the first item as היראה (fear) and in the second as כעס (anger); the Midrashic source (Genesis Rabbah 59:8) lists five items including רשות (authority/fear of kingship). The text here is rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:39.750826479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}